WEBVTT

1
00:00:03.400 --> 00:00:07.820
You cut both copies of a gene in every cell of a dish.

2
00:00:08.600 --> 00:00:11.360
Why is half the dish still making the protein?

3
00:00:12.320 --> 00:00:17.920
Look at one cell. It comes from a diploid line, so it carries two copies of the

4
00:00:17.920 --> 00:00:21.500
gene. We aim Cas9 at both copies.

5
00:00:22.160 --> 00:00:23.740
The cut is the easy part.

6
00:00:24.340 --> 00:00:26.720
What the cell does next is the real question.

7
00:00:27.583 --> 00:00:30.143
Cas9 is aimed by a guide.

8
00:00:31.023 --> 00:00:34.523
The target-binding part of the guide is 20 nucleotides long.

9
00:00:35.203 --> 00:00:39.123
It must sit right next to a short motif called the PAM.

10
00:00:39.803 --> 00:00:41.883
The PAM reads NGG.

11
00:00:43.223 --> 00:00:47.843
Cas9 ignored DNA that matched the guide but had no nearby PAM.

12
00:00:48.603 --> 00:00:52.923
In the original test, the cut fell three base pairs upstream of the PAM.

13
00:00:53.783 --> 00:00:56.683
The cut is where your control ends.

14
00:00:57.303 --> 00:00:59.463
The cell repairs the break on its own.

15
00:01:00.303 --> 00:01:01.823
One route is end joining.

16
00:01:02.343 --> 00:01:03.423
It needs no template.

17
00:01:03.883 --> 00:01:06.183
It can leave insertions and deletions.

18
00:01:07.103 --> 00:01:09.743
Another route is homology-directed repair.

19
00:01:10.363 --> 00:01:12.363
It copies a template that you supply.

20
00:01:13.163 --> 00:01:16.563
So sequencing the pool shows a spread of different alleles.

21
00:01:17.317 --> 00:01:19.837
Now count the alleles in the pool.

22
00:01:20.697 --> 00:01:22.397
10% are unedited.

23
00:01:23.197 --> 00:01:25.597
35% lose one base pair.

24
00:01:26.217 --> 00:01:34.977
25% gain one. 15% lose three, 5% lose six, and 10% lose seven.

25
00:01:35.717 --> 00:01:39.577
A length that is not a multiple of three shifts the frame.

26
00:01:40.417 --> 00:01:44.197
Lose one, gain one and lose seven all shift it.

27
00:01:44.957 --> 00:01:48.277
Add them up. 70% are frameshifted.

28
00:01:49.117 --> 00:01:51.537
Each cell has two alleles.

29
00:01:52.077 --> 00:01:53.937
Assume they are edited independently.

30
00:01:54.897 --> 00:01:58.677
The chance that one allele is frameshifted is 0.7.

31
00:01:59.337 --> 00:02:00.797
For both, multiply.

32
00:02:01.877 --> 00:02:05.857
0.7 times 0.7 is 0.49.

33
00:02:06.577 --> 00:02:10.377
So 49% of cells have both alleles frameshifted.

34
00:02:11.237 --> 00:02:16.337
About half the cells still carry at least one in-frame or unedited allele.

35
00:02:17.200 --> 00:02:24.220
So a cut, a mutated allele, and a knockout are three different states.

36
00:02:25.240 --> 00:02:27.700
Only the third is a genetic result.

37
00:02:28.700 --> 00:02:32.220
A pool like this is a mosaic, not a null.

38
00:02:33.420 --> 00:02:39.320
Derive clones and genotype them, or use more than one guide, before you call the

39
00:02:39.320 --> 00:02:40.540
phenotype a knockout.
